WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.840
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.889
Today we are going to see how NVIDIA’s crazy
new AI is able to understand basically any

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:13.209
human movement.

00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:16.300
So, how is this even possible?

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.620
And even more importantly, what is pose estimation?

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:26.700
Well, simple, a video of people goes in, and
the posture they are taking comes out.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.460
Now, you see here that previous techniques
can already do this quite well, what’s more,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.890
if we allow an AI to read the wifi-signals
bouncing around in a room, it can perform

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.579
pose estimation, even through walls.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:43.579
Kind of.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:47.390
Now, you may be wondering what is pose estimation
good for?

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.320
By the end of this video, you will see that
this can help us move around in virtual worlds,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.100
the metaverse, if you will.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:00.590
But let’s not rush there yet, because still,
there are several key challenges here.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:06.120
One, we need to be super accurate to even
put a dent into this problem.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.120
Why?

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.280
Well, because if we have a video, and we are
off by just by a tiny bit from frame to frame,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.409
this kind of flickering may happen.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.670
That’s a challenge.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.320
What else is challenging here?

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.680
Two, foot sliding.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.220
Yes, you heard it right.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.560
Yes, previous methods suffer from this phenomenon,
you can see it in action here.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.540
And also, here too.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.260
So, why does this happen?

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:37.080
It happens because the technique has no knowledge
of the physics of real human movements.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:43.410
So, scientists at NVIDIA, the University of
Toronto and the Vector Institute fired up

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:49.009
a collaboration, and when I first heard about
their concept, I thought you are doing what?

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:51.140
But, check this out.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.460
First, they perform a regular pose estimation.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:00.579
Of course, this is no good, as it has the
dreaded temporal inconsistency, or in other

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:02.210
words, flickering.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.390
And in other cases, often foot sliding too.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.580
Now, hold on to your papers, because here
comes the magic!

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:15.970
Now, they transfer the motion to a video game
character, and embed that character in a physics

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.120
simulation.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.140
In this virtual world, the motion can be corrected
to make sure they are physically correct.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:29.200
Now, remember, foot sliding happens because
of the lack of knowledge in physics, so, perhaps

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.379
this idea is not that crazy after all!

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:33.870
Let’s have a look!

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.450
Now this will be quite a challenge.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.239
Explosive sprinting motions.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:40.750
And…whoa.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.290
This is amazing.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:49.670
This, dear Fellow Scholars, is superb pose
estimation and tracking.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:50.670
How about this?

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:57.180
A good tennis serve includes lots of dynamic
motion, and just look at how beautifully it

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.090
reconstructs this move.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.240
Apparently, physics works.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:06.879
Now, the output also needn’t be stickmen,
we can retarget these to proper textured virtual

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:09.440
characters built from a triangle mesh.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.720
And, that’s just one step away from us being
able to appear in a metaverse.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.660
No head mounted displays are required, no
expensive studio, no motion capture equipment

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.739
is required.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:25.150
So, what IS required?

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.410
Actually, nothing!

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.770
Just a raw input video of us.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.120
That is insanity.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.250
And all this produces physically correct motion.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:41.200
So, that crazy idea about taking people and
transforming them into video game characters

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.590
is not so crazy after all.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:49.650
So now, we are one step closer to be able
to work, and even have some coffee together

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.980
in a virtual world.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:04:09.920
What a 

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:22.839
time 

00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:52.380
to 
be alive!

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.789
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

